WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:01.970
Is a whale a fish?

00:00:01.970 --> 00:00:03.795
No, a whale is a mammal.

00:00:03.795 --> 00:00:06.532
That answer came from a transformer running under MS-DOS.

00:00:06.912 --> 00:00:10.151
So did this one: seventeen times twenty-three is ninety-six.

00:00:10.151 --> 00:00:10.871
It isn't.

00:00:13.710 --> 00:00:14.774
Hi, I'm Doskey.

00:00:14.774 --> 00:00:19.384
Tiny is a small GPT that fits next to DOS in conventional memory.

00:00:19.384 --> 00:00:22.576
Its tricks are the same ones modern runtimes use.

00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:26.167
There is no tokenizer.

00:00:26.167 --> 00:00:31.260
Every printable character is a token, so the whole vocabulary is ninety-seven entries.

00:00:31.660 --> 00:00:35.873
Each letter gets a token embedding plus a learned position, out of

00:00:35.873 --> 00:00:37.277
a context of 128.

00:00:37.677 --> 00:00:41.389
Four layers of attention and a small ReLU block, with RMS

00:00:41.389 --> 00:00:42.739
norms and no biases.

00:00:43.139 --> 00:00:47.043
The output reuses the token embedding, so one table does two jobs.

00:00:47.443 --> 00:00:49.933
Then it picks a letter, and feeds it back in.

00:00:49.933 --> 00:00:51.675
Every answer letter is a full pass.

00:00:53.592 --> 00:00:54.844
Weights are signed bytes.

00:00:54.844 --> 00:00:59.224
But the sum is a float, so the multiply-add runs on the math coprocessor.

00:00:59.644 --> 00:01:02.945
The row's scale is applied once, after the dot product.

00:01:02.945 --> 00:01:06.907
That is why it wants a 486DX, and crawls on an SX.

00:01:08.092 --> 00:01:09.741
I ran it in DOSBox-X.

00:01:09.741 --> 00:01:12.710
Three answers took under a minute with the coprocessor.

00:01:12.710 --> 00:01:16.997
With it switched off, the software emulator took about thirty-one times as long.

00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:19.190
Add it up.

00:01:19.190 --> 00:01:23.047
The weight file, an eight-bit cache of keys and values, and the program.

00:01:23.047 --> 00:01:27.201
That is most of the five hundred and forty kilobytes the README asks for.

00:01:28.908 --> 00:01:29.771
And it talks.

00:01:29.771 --> 00:01:33.798
The PC speaker can play one square wave, at one pitch, and nothing else.

00:01:34.218 --> 00:01:37.959
So for a vowel, it flips between two pitches every two milliseconds,

00:01:37.959 --> 00:01:39.829
and your ear blurs them together.

00:01:40.249 --> 00:01:43.585
S and F become hiss, a new random pitch every millisecond.

00:01:43.585 --> 00:01:45.707
P and T are a tiny click.

00:01:46.127 --> 00:01:49.541
String those together with the rhythm of speech, and you get a film robot.

00:01:52.608 --> 00:01:55.819
On questions it never saw, it gets sixty-one percent right.

00:01:56.239 --> 00:01:59.693
Its arithmetic stops at small sums and the twelve times table.

00:02:00.113 --> 00:02:03.303
Outside its facts, it invents answers with full confidence.

00:02:04.558 --> 00:02:06.096
The model is a toy.

00:02:06.096 --> 00:02:09.788
The engineering is not: store in bytes, compute in floats, quantize the

00:02:09.788 --> 00:02:11.327
cache, and count every byte.

00:02:12.983 --> 00:02:16.858
Letters as tokens, bytes for weights, and a vowel made of two pitches.

00:02:16.858 --> 00:02:17.753
Thanks for watching!

00:02:18.173 --> 00:02:20.307
Every source is in the full article.

00:02:20.307 --> 00:02:20.917
I'm Doskey.

00:02:20.917 --> 00:02:21.222
Bye!
